  "notes": "Carolina bounce-trap, and the whole model turns on one device the research names outright: a **semitone-neighbour ostinato over a static tonic** rather than a progression. That is why `melody.halfStepDissonance` is 0.35 — the highest thing on this bench — with `motifPitchCount` 2 and `monophonicBias` 0.95 beside it, because \"one sound brought it\" is a stated working rule and layering would erase the device. The hats are the other signature: `continuous` with `fillDensity` 0.85, an unwavering straight 16th carpet, against a kick that pushes on the 8ths (`offbeat8thShare` 0.35) and a punchy short 808 with almost no glide, leaving the offbeats open. Open hats and a `ride` lane carry his stated live-church-drummer background. ⚠ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set because the research describes a static tonic with sample-flip cuts, not a progression — the two-chord families below carry a low weight and are INFERRED, present so the bass has more than one root to reach and the chord part can still make its distinct-output floor. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
